          <span>{item.watch}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>The degree is decided. The next question is whether the next stretch of time creates
      proof or only waiting.</strong> Whatever your situation, a lane is worth choosing only when you
      can name what you will build, write or ship in it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="lane-cards">The seven lanes, ordered by income headroom</h2>

  <p>
    The order follows how far income can realistically grow with skill and proof, not prestige or
    exam difficulty. Consulting leads because you set the price, even though it is the slowest to
    start. Government sits last because its pay bands are fixed, and it is still a fine choice for the
    right person: stable pay is a real benefit.
  </p>

  <div class="lane-grid">
    {laneCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="lane-card">
        <span class="lane-card__badge">{item.badge}</span>
        <div class="lane-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
        <div class="card-subrow card-subrow--positive">
          <span class="card-subrow__label">Best for</span>
          <span>{item.best}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="card-subrow card-subrow--caution">
          <span class="card-subrow__label">Watch out</span>
          <span>{item.watch}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Consulting and independent technical work are the lanes where income scales, because you set the
      price and can hire or package the work. They also need referrals and proof, which take time. For
      most engineers the practical route is a job in a stronger lane, plus one small paid project on
      the side until it can carry weight of its own.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    If consulting or freelancing is one of your two lanes, three habits protect you early. Price a
    fixed-scope package for the result, not the hours. Get scope, price, timeline and revision limits
    in writing, with a deposit on larger jobs. And check GST and Udyam registration rules on the
    official portals before your turnover grows, since thresholds and rules change.
  </p>

  <p>
    Note where competition stacks. A lane can have a hard entry filter and a second hard filter later:
    GATE and then the PSU round, CAT and then college interviews and placements, a competitive M.Tech
    seat and then a scarce research role. Clearing the first filter does not clear the second, so plan
    for both.
  </p>

